// =====================================================================
// P&L HIERARCHY - CALCULATED COLUMNS ON TBData
// =====================================================================
// Builds the 3 visible columns of the P&L matrix. Each level pulls from a
// different source table depending on which Level 1 section the row is in.
//
//   Level 1  = "Revenue"  if TBData[LineItem] = "Revenue"
//              "COGS"     if LineItem = "COGS" and TBData[Segment] < 116
//              "OPEX"     if TBData[Account Desc] is in OPEXMap[CTDesc]
//              else LineItem, or "Unmapped" when that is blank
//   Level 2  = driven by TBData[CC] alone: 991-995 revenue roll-ups,
//              then Matrix 286 -> "Bonus", then three OPEXMap CTDesc Parents
//              -> "G&A", then CCMap[Primary Group], then the fixed
//              "nnn-Description" code list
//   Level 3  = LineItem & " " & RevMap[PLSect_cleaned]  when InternalRevMap
//                                                       is in RevMap[RevIndex]
//              COGSMap[CTDesc Parent]  when Account Desc is in COGSMap[CTDesc]
//              CCMap[CostCenter]       when Cc is in CCMap[CCCode]
//              ...preceded by OPEXMap[CTDesc Parent] for the three G&A parents
//   Level 4  = RevMap[FPA Mapping]     when InternalRevMap is in RevMap[SegMtx]
//              OPEXMap[CTDesc Parent]  when Account Desc is in OPEXMap[CTDesc]
//
// NO RELATIONSHIPS REQUIRED:
//   Every cross-table read uses LOOKUPVALUE with an explicit key, and every
//   membership test uses IN ALL(). None of the three columns depends on a
//   relationship existing, or on which way its arrow points. Add the
//   many-to-one relationships and swap LOOKUPVALUE for RELATED() only if you
//   want the speed on a large trial balance.
// =====================================================================


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. LEVEL 1  -- Revenue / COGS / OPEX
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Four gates, tested strictly in this order. The first one that matches wins:
//
//   1. LineItem = "Revenue"                        -> "Revenue"
//   2. LineItem = "COGS" AND Segment < 116         -> "COGS"
//   3. Account Desc found in OPEXMap[CTDesc]       -> "OPEX"
//   4. otherwise                                   -> LineItem
//      ...and if LineItem is blank                 -> "Unmapped"
//
// Note what gate 2 does: a COGS row with Segment >= 116 does NOT stop there.
// It falls through to gate 3, where it can be reclassified as OPEX. If it is
// not in OPEXMap either, gate 4 returns "COGS" anyway (that is its LineItem).
// So the Segment test is a gate that lets high-segment COGS rows escape into
// OPEX -- it never loses a row.
//
// LineItem, Segment and Account Desc are all columns ON TBData, and the OPEX
// test uses ALL(), so this column needs NO relationships at all.
//
// This column produces ONLY Revenue / COGS / OPEX (plus any other LineItem
// value and "Unmapped"). Adjusted EBITDA, Gross Profit and Total Revenue
// cannot appear here -- they are arithmetic across sections, not labels on
// trial-balance rows. See the last section of this file.

PL Level 1 =
VAR _LineItem = TBData[LineItem]
VAR _IsOpex =
    TBData[Account Desc] IN ALL ( OPEXMap[CTDesc] )
RETURN
    SWITCH (
        TRUE (),
        _LineItem = "Revenue",                        "Revenue",
        _LineItem = "COGS" && TBData[Segment] < 116,  "COGS",
        _IsOpex,                                      "OPEX",
        COALESCE ( _LineItem, "Unmapped" )
    )


// -- THE OPEX MEMBERSHIP TEST
//    "IN ALL ( OPEXMap[CTDesc] )" asks: does this row's Account Desc appear
//    ANYWHERE in the OPEXMap[CTDesc] column? ALL() is used rather than
//    VALUES() so the test ignores filter context and any relationship that
//    may exist between TBData and OPEXMap. No relationship is required.
//
//    Equivalent if you prefer it spelled out:
//        VAR _IsOpex = CONTAINS ( OPEXMap, OPEXMap[CTDesc], TBData[Account Desc] )


// -- TYPE NOTES (both of these fail QUIETLY -- check them before trusting the
//    output):
//
//    Segment: "< 116" assumes a NUMBER. If Segment is stored as Text, DAX
//    either errors or compares alphabetically ("99" > "116" as text), so the
//    COGS gate misfires. Column tools -> Data type. If it is text, convert:
//        VALUE ( TBData[Segment] ) < 116
//    and make sure no blanks or non-numeric values exist first, or VALUE
//    throws.
//
//    Account Desc vs CTDesc: these must match EXACTLY -- same trailing
//    spaces, same casing conventions. Trim both columns in Power Query before
//    relying on this. A single trailing space silently drops a row out of
//    OPEX and into gate 4.
//
//    Is the boundary right? "< 116" excludes 116 itself. If Segment 116
//    should count as COGS, use "<= 116".
//
//    Column naming: written as TBData[LineItem]. If the column is actually
//    "Line Item" with a space, adjust -- DAX will flag that one as an error
//    rather than failing quietly.


// -- DIAGNOSTIC: two throwaway columns that show the conditional gates
//    separately. Delete them once Level 1 checks out.
//
//    Debug Segment  = TBData[Segment] < 116
//    Debug InOpex   = TBData[Account Desc] IN ALL ( OPEXMap[CTDesc] )
//
//    Put these next to TBData[LineItem] and PL Level 1 in a table visual with
//    [Amount]. Rows landing on "Unmapped" have a blank LineItem AND no
//    OPEXMap match -- that is the list to hand back to Finance.


// -- NOTE ON BellcorpMap / BellPLMap
//    LineItem is read straight off TBData, so the two-hop bridge
//    TBData -> BellPLMap -> BellcorpMap is no longer used by this report.
//    Neither are the relationships it needed. Leave the tables in the model
//    if other reports depend on them; nothing here does.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. LEVEL 2  -- the blue column
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Driven entirely by TBData[CC]. Four gates, first match wins:
//
//   1. Cc 991-995                -> the five revenue roll-up labels
//   2. Matrix = 286              -> "Bonus"          (see BONUS note below)
//   3. OPEXMap[CTDesc Parent] is Bad Debt & Collection / Telecommunications /
//      Taxes & Regulatory Fees   -> "G&A"            (see G&A note below)
//   4. Cc found in CCMap[CCCode] -> CCMap[Primary Group]
//   5. Cc in the fixed code list -> "nnn-Description"
//   6. otherwise                 -> BLANK            (see FALLBACK note)
//
// This no longer branches on PL Level 1 -- the CC value alone decides the
// label, so Revenue / COGS / OPEX rows all flow through the same gates.
//
// NOTE: SWITCH stops at the FIRST matching condition, so listing a condition
// twice makes the second copy unreachable. Each gate below appears exactly
// once -- if you paste a duplicated "_InCCMap, _CCGroup," line back in, it is
// dead code: harmless, never evaluated, but worth deleting so the gate list
// stays readable.

PL Level 2 =
VAR _Cc = TBData[CC]
VAR _InCCMap = _Cc IN ALL ( CCMap[CCCode] )
VAR _OpexParent =
    LOOKUPVALUE (
        OPEXMap[CTDesc Parent],
        OPEXMap[CTDesc], TBData[Account Desc]
    )
VAR _CCGroup =
    LOOKUPVALUE (
        CCMap[Primary Group],
        CCMap[CCCode], _Cc
    )
VAR _CodeLabel =
    SWITCH (
        _Cc,
        1,   "001-Other Carrier Network - Special Circuits",
        2,   "002-Other Carrier Network - Switched",
        11,  "011-Long Distance",
        21,  "021-Resale Fee",
        22,  "022",
        24,  "024",
        25,  "025-Consumer Other COGS",
        27,  "027-FiOS COGS",
        31,  "031-Large Systems",
        32,  "032-Small Systems",
        78,  "078-Miscellaneous Network",
        79,  "079-Pole and Conduit Rentals",
        97,  "097-SS7 Circuit",
        106, "106-Backbone Network",
        116, "116-DIPs"
    )
RETURN
    SWITCH (
        TRUE (),
        // 1. revenue roll-ups
        _Cc = 991, "Total Residential Revenue",
        _Cc = 992, "Total SMB Revenue",
        _Cc = 993, "Total Enterprise Revenue",
        _Cc = 994, "Total Wholesale Revenue",
        _Cc = 995, "Total NFC Revenue",
        // 2. bonus override
        TBData[Matrix] = 286, "Bonus",
        // 3. OPEX parents that roll up into G&A
        _OpexParent IN {
            "Bad Debt & Collection",
            "Telecommunications",
            "Taxes & Regulatory Fees"
        }, "G&A",
        // 4. mapped cost centre
        _InCCMap, _CCGroup,
        // 5. fixed network / COGS code list
        NOT ISBLANK ( _CodeLabel ), _CodeLabel,
        // 6. nothing matched
        BLANK ()
    )


// -- BONUS NOTE (please confirm)
//    You did not restate the Matrix = 286 -> "Bonus" rule in the new spec, but
//    you asked for it earlier and have not retracted it, so it is kept. It
//    sits at gate 2: after the revenue roll-ups (a compensation code should
//    never outrank a revenue total) and before CCMap, because CCMap is what
//    supplies the department label that Bonus is meant to override.
//    Delete that one line if bonus should now come through CCMap like any
//    other cost centre.


// -- G&A ROLL-UP NOTE
//    _OpexParent uses the OPEXMap <-> TBData join (Account Desc <-> CTDesc),
//    the same one Level 1 uses to decide OPEX and Level 4 uses for its second
//    gate. It returns BLANK for any row not in OPEXMap, so this gate is
//    inherently OPEX-scoped -- no extra "is this OPEX" test is needed.
//
//    ORDER MATTERS: it sits ahead of the CCMap gate on purpose. Behind it,
//    CCMap[Primary Group] would already have claimed these rows and the
//    reclass would never fire.
//
//    It sits AFTER Bonus, so a Matrix 286 row stays "Bonus" even if its
//    CTDesc Parent is one of the three. Swap the two gates if G&A should win
//    instead -- but check first whether any row satisfies both.
//
//    CONSEQUENCE: these three parents leave their own cost-centre groups, so
//    those groups' subtotals shrink by exactly this amount. Intended when
//    Finance reports them within G&A; worth confirming, because the affected
//    department will no longer tie to its own ledger.
//
//    EXACT STRINGS: "Bad Debt & Collection", "Telecommunications" and
//    "Taxes & Regulatory Fees" must match OPEXMap character for character,
//    ampersands included. Trim OPEXMap[CTDesc Parent] in Power Query, then
//    confirm with a table visual of DISTINCT CTDesc Parent values that these
//    three appear exactly as written.
//
//    Rows reaching this gate already have "G&A" available as a CCMap Primary
//    Group value, so both routes produce the same label and PL Level 2 Sort
//    treats them identically.


// -- FALLBACK NOTE
//    Gate 6 returns BLANK exactly as specified. A blank Level 2 renders as an
//    empty row header in the matrix, which makes those amounts hard to find
//    and easy to overlook in reconciliation. To surface them instead:
//        BLANK ()   ->   "Unmapped Cc " & _Cc
//    That matches how Level 1 handles its own misses and costs nothing.


// -- DUPLICATE KEYS IN CCMap
//    LOOKUPVALUE ERRORS OUT if CCMap holds more than one row for the same
//    CCCode -- "A table of multiple values was supplied". This is the same
//    dedupe problem as the Name / Set Number CSV: in Power Query, select
//    CCCode -> Home -> Remove Rows -> Remove Duplicates, after a Trim. Verify
//    first with Group By on CCCode filtered to Count > 1, so you can see
//    which rows you would be dropping rather than letting Power Query pick.


// -- TYPE NOTE
//    Every Cc literal above is written as a NUMBER, matching the right-aligned
//    numeric codes in your source. If Cc is stored as TEXT, none of the
//    comparisons match and every row falls to gate 5 -- quote them all
//    ( _Cc = "991", 1 becomes "1" ... ) and check whether the text values
//    carry leading zeros ("001" rather than "1"), which changes the literals
//    again. Column tools -> Data type settles it.


// -- MAINTAINABILITY
//    The 15-entry code list is fine hard-coded while it is stable. If it
//    starts changing, move it to a two-column reference table (Cc, Label) and
//    replace _CodeLabel with a LOOKUPVALUE against it -- one table to edit
//    instead of DAX.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. LEVEL 3  -- the red column
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Three membership gates, first match wins:
//
//   1. OPEXMap[CTDesc Parent] is one of the three G&A parents
//         -> that CTDesc Parent
//   2. InternalRevMap found in RevMap[RevIndex]
//         -> TBData[LineItem] & " " & RevMap[PLSect_cleaned]
//   3. Account Desc found in COGSMap[CTDesc]
//         -> COGSMap[CTDesc Parent]
//   4. Cc found in CCMap[CCCode]
//         -> CCMap[CostCenter]
//   5. otherwise -> BLANK
//
// Like Level 2, this does not branch on PL Level 1. Each gate is a membership
// test against a different map table, so the row's own keys decide the label.

PL Level 3 =
VAR _OpexParent =
    LOOKUPVALUE (
        OPEXMap[CTDesc Parent],
        OPEXMap[CTDesc], TBData[Account Desc]
    )
VAR _IsGA =
    _OpexParent IN {
        "Bad Debt & Collection",
        "Telecommunications",
        "Taxes & Regulatory Fees"
    }
VAR _RevSect =
    LOOKUPVALUE (
        RevMap[PLSect_cleaned],
        RevMap[RevIndex], TBData[InternalRevMap]
    )
VAR _CogsParent =
    LOOKUPVALUE (
        COGSMap[CTDesc Parent],
        COGSMap[CTDesc], TBData[Account Desc]
    )
VAR _CostCenter =
    LOOKUPVALUE (
        CCMap[CostCenter],
        CCMap[CCCode], TBData[CC]
    )
RETURN
    SWITCH (
        TRUE (),
        // 1. the G&A roll-up broken back out by its parent
        _IsGA, _OpexParent,
        // 2. revenue -- LineItem prefixed onto the cleaned P&L section
        TBData[InternalRevMap] IN ALL ( RevMap[RevIndex] ),
            TRIM ( TBData[LineItem] & " " & _RevSect ),
        // 3. COGS -- parent description
        TBData[Account Desc] IN ALL ( COGSMap[CTDesc] ),
            _CogsParent,
        // 4. OPEX -- cost centre
        TBData[CC] IN ALL ( CCMap[CCCode] ),
            _CostCenter,
        // 5. nothing matched
        BLANK ()
    )


// -- WHY THE G&A GATE IS FIRST
//    It guarantees the pairing: every row Level 2 labels "G&A" gets its
//    CTDesc Parent here, with no exceptions. Placed lower, a G&A row that also
//    appears in COGSMap or RevMap would be claimed by one of those gates and
//    Level 3 would contradict Level 2 for that row. Being first costs nothing
//    -- the gate only fires when OPEXMap returns one of the three exact
//    strings, which no revenue or COGS row can do.
//
//    So under the "G&A" row in the matrix, Level 3 shows the three parents
//    (Bad Debt & Collection, Telecommunications, Taxes & Regulatory Fees) as
//    its children -- the roll-up broken back out.
//
//    MAINTENANCE: the three strings now appear in BOTH PL Level 2 and
//    PL Level 3. If the list ever changes, edit both -- or move it to a small
//    one-column reference table and test with IN ALL ( GAParents[Parent] ).


// -- WHY TRIM ON GATE 2
//    "A & " " & B" in DAX treats a BLANK operand as an empty string, so a
//    blank LineItem or a blank PLSect_cleaned would leave a stray leading or
//    trailing space -- and " Resi Data" sorts and groups as a DIFFERENT value
//    from "Resi Data" in the matrix. TRIM removes it. Note the gate only fires
//    when InternalRevMap IS in RevIndex, so a blank PLSect_cleaned means the
//    RevMap row exists but its label is empty -- worth chasing in the source
//    rather than shipping a bare LineItem.


// -- DUPLICATE KEYS: THREE TABLES TO CHECK
//    Every gate uses LOOKUPVALUE, which ERRORS on duplicate keys rather than
//    picking one ("A table of multiple values was supplied"). Dedupe all three
//    before this column will evaluate:
//        RevMap   on RevIndex
//        COGSMap  on CTDesc
//        CCMap    on CCCode
//    In Power Query: Trim the key column, then Group By it filtered to
//    Count > 1 to see what is duplicated, then Remove Duplicates.


// -- NOTE: Account Desc is now doing double duty
//    Level 1 tests it against OPEXMap[CTDesc] to decide OPEX.
//    Level 3 tests it against COGSMap[CTDesc] to fetch the COGS parent.
//    Two different tables, both with a column called CTDesc. If a single
//    Account Desc appears in BOTH map tables, that row is classified OPEX at
//    Level 1 while Level 3 labels it with a COGS parent -- a visible
//    contradiction in the matrix. Worth checking once:
//        table visual of TBData[Account Desc] filtered to values present in
//        both OPEXMap[CTDesc] and COGSMap[CTDesc]; the overlap should be empty.


// -- TYPE NOTES
//    InternalRevMap vs RevIndex, and Cc vs CCCode, must be the SAME data type
//    on both sides. A whole number matched against text never matches, and
//    fails silently -- the row just falls to the next gate. Column tools ->
//    Data type on each of the four columns.
//
//    Column naming: written as CCMap[CostCenter] (no space), per your latest
//    spec. Earlier it appeared as "Cost Center". DAX errors on a wrong column
//    name rather than failing quietly, so paste will tell you.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. LEVEL 4  -- the green detail column
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Two membership gates, first match wins:
//
//   1. InternalRevMap found in RevMap[SegMtx]   -> RevMap[FPA Mapping]
//   2. Account Desc found in OPEXMap[CTDesc]    -> OPEXMap[CTDesc Parent]
//   3. otherwise                                -> BLANK
//
// Gate 2 fires on exactly the rows Level 1 classified as OPEX -- it is the
// same membership test, so the two stay consistent for free.

PL Level 4 =
VAR _Idx  = TBData[InternalRevMap]
VAR _Desc = TBData[Account Desc]
VAR _FPA =
    LOOKUPVALUE (
        RevMap[FPA Mapping],
        RevMap[SegMtx], _Idx
    )
VAR _OpexParent =
    LOOKUPVALUE (
        OPEXMap[CTDesc Parent],
        OPEXMap[CTDesc], _Desc
    )
RETURN
    SWITCH (
        TRUE (),
        _Idx  IN ALL ( RevMap[SegMtx] ),   _FPA,
        _Desc IN ALL ( OPEXMap[CTDesc] ),  _OpexParent,
        BLANK ()
    )


// -- WORTH CONFIRMING: SegMtx vs RevIndex
//    Level 3 matches TBData[InternalRevMap] against RevMap[RevIndex].
//    Level 4 matches the SAME TBData column against RevMap[SegMtx].
//    Two different key columns in RevMap, one source column in TBData. That
//    is plausible if RevMap carries two index columns at different grains,
//    but it is also exactly what a typo looks like. Check RevMap in Data view:
//    if SegMtx and RevIndex hold the same values, either column works and the
//    distinction does not matter; if they differ, make sure Level 4 is meant
//    to key off the coarser one.


// -- DUPLICATE KEYS
//    Both gates use LOOKUPVALUE, which ERRORS on duplicate keys rather than
//    picking one. OPEXMap was membership-only until now -- from this column on
//    it must ALSO be unique on CTDesc. Two more tables/keys to dedupe:
//        RevMap   on SegMtx
//        OPEXMap  on CTDesc
//
//    If a column will not evaluate because of duplicates and you want to see
//    output before cleaning the source, swap either LOOKUPVALUE for the
//    non-erroring form:
//        VAR _FPA =
//            MAXX (
//                FILTER ( ALL ( RevMap ), RevMap[SegMtx] = _Idx ),
//                RevMap[FPA Mapping]
//            )
//    MAXX collapses multiple matches instead of throwing. It HIDES the data
//    problem -- if two rows disagree you silently get the alphabetically later
//    value -- so treat it as a diagnostic, not a fix.


// -- TYPE NOTE
//    InternalRevMap and SegMtx must be the same data type. A whole number
//    matched against text never matches and raises no error: gate 1 simply
//    never fires and every revenue row falls through to gate 2 or to BLANK.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. SORT COLUMNS -- so the matrix renders Revenue, COGS, OPEX in order
//    Set each PL Level n column's "Sort by column" to its matching Sort col.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
PL Level 1 Sort =
SWITCH (
    TBData[PL Level 1],
    "Revenue", 10,
    "COGS",    30,
    "OPEX",    50,
    99
)

// Level 2 sort: revenue roll-ups, then the fixed code list in Cc order, then
// CCMap primary groups, then Bonus last.
//
// IMPORTANT -- this depends ONLY on the Level 2 label, never on Cc or on
// PL Level 1 Sort. Power BI's "Sort by column" requires exactly ONE sort value
// per displayed value. Several Cc codes can map to the same CCMap primary
// group, so keying the sort off Cc would give one label two sort values and
// Power BI would reject it with "there can't be more than one value in
// <sort column> for the same value in <column>". Level 1 is already the outer
// row level, so it does the section grouping on its own.

PL Level 2 Sort =
SWITCH (
    TBData[PL Level 2],
    // revenue roll-ups
    "Total Residential Revenue",                       1,
    "Total SMB Revenue",                               2,
    "Total Enterprise Revenue",                        3,
    "Total Wholesale Revenue",                         4,
    "Total NFC Revenue",                               5,
    // fixed code list, ordered by Cc (100 + code)
    "001-Other Carrier Network - Special Circuits",  101,
    "002-Other Carrier Network - Switched",          102,
    "011-Long Distance",                             111,
    "021-Resale Fee",                                121,
    "022",                                           122,
    "024",                                           124,
    "025-Consumer Other COGS",                       125,
    "027-FiOS COGS",                                 127,
    "031-Large Systems",                             131,
    "032-Small Systems",                             132,
    "078-Miscellaneous Network",                     178,
    "079-Pole and Conduit Rentals",                  179,
    "097-SS7 Circuit",                               197,
    "106-Backbone Network",                          206,
    "116-DIPs",                                      216,
    // Bonus always last
    "Bonus",                                         999,
    // "G&A" is not listed: it arrives either as a CCMap Primary Group or via
    // the CTDesc Parent roll-up, and both routes produce the same label, so it
    // falls to the shared 500 alongside the other primary groups.
    // everything else -- CCMap primary groups -- sits between the code list
    // and Bonus, alphabetically among themselves
    500
)


// =====================================================================
// MEASURES
// =====================================================================
// SIGN CONVENTION: this assumes costs are stored as POSITIVE numbers in
// TBData[Amount]. If your trial balance carries costs as NEGATIVES (credit
// convention), every subtraction below becomes an addition:
//     Adjusted EBITDA := [Total Revenue] + [Total COGS] + [Total OPEX]
// Check one COGS row in Data view before trusting these numbers.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

Amount := SUM ( TBData[Amount] )

Total Revenue :=
CALCULATE ( [Amount], TBData[PL Level 1] = "Revenue" )

Total COGS :=
CALCULATE ( [Amount], TBData[PL Level 1] = "COGS" )

Gross Profit :=
[Total Revenue] - [Total COGS]

Total OPEX :=
CALCULATE ( [Amount], TBData[PL Level 1] = "OPEX" )

Adjusted EBITDA :=
[Total Revenue] - [Total COGS] - [Total OPEX]

Gross Margin % :=
DIVIDE ( [Gross Profit], [Total Revenue] )

Adjusted EBITDA Margin % :=
DIVIDE ( [Adjusted EBITDA], [Total Revenue] )

// D&A CAVEAT: EBITDA excludes Depreciation and Amortisation. The formula
// above is Revenue - COGS - OPEX exactly as specified, so if any D&A accounts
// sit inside the 12 OPEX departments, this is operating income, not EBITDA.
// To exclude them, filter the accounts out of Total OPEX, e.g.
//     Total OPEX :=
//     CALCULATE (
//         [Amount],
//         TBData[PL Level 1] = "OPEX",
//         NOT TBData[Account] IN { <D&A account codes> }
//     )
// Confirm with Finance which accounts are in scope before changing this.


// =====================================================================
// ADJUSTED EBITDA AS A ROW IN THE MATRIX
// =====================================================================
// IMPORTANT -- why this is NOT another value in the PL Level 1 column:
//
// PL Level 1 is a CALCULATED COLUMN. Every value it produces is a label
// stamped onto an actual trial-balance row. Adjusted EBITDA is not a set of
// rows -- it is arithmetic ACROSS three sets of rows. No TB row "is" Adjusted
// EBITDA, so there is nothing for the column to label. Adding
// "Adjusted EBITDA" to the SWITCH in PL Level 1 is not possible: it would
// have to steal rows from Revenue, COGS or OPEX, and those rows are already
// counted once in their own section. The same is true of Total Revenue and
// Gross Profit.
//
// Calculated rows must therefore come from a disconnected LAYOUT table that
// supplies the row headers, with a measure deciding what each row evaluates.
// Pick ONE of the two approaches below.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// APPROACH A (recommended to start) -- two visuals
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Keep the plain hierarchy matrix (PL Level 1 > 2 > 3, subtotals on) and put
// [Gross Profit] and [Adjusted EBITDA] in Card visuals or a small one-row
// matrix directly beneath it. Nothing further to build -- the measures above
// are all you need. This is what most production P&L reports ship, and it is
// always arithmetically correct.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// APPROACH B -- one matrix with Adjusted EBITDA as a real row
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Gives the exact sketch layout. The row headers come from PL Layout instead
// of from PL Level 1.

PL Layout =
DATATABLE (
    "Sort",     INTEGER,
    "PL Line",  STRING,
    "Row Type", STRING,
    {
        { 10, "Revenue",         "Section" },
        { 20, "Total Revenue",   "Calc"    },
        { 30, "COGS",            "Section" },
        { 40, "Gross Profit",    "Calc"    },
        { 50, "OPEX",            "Section" },
        { 60, "Adjusted EBITDA", "Calc"    }
    }
)

// Setup:
//   1. Leave PL Layout DISCONNECTED -- no relationship to anything.
//   2. Sort PL Line by Sort (select PL Line -> Column tools -> Sort by column).
//   3. Matrix Rows: 'PL Layout'[PL Line], then TBData[PL Level 2],
//      then TBData[PL Level 3].
//   4. Matrix Values: [PL Value] (below), NOT [Amount].
//   5. Format pane -> turn "Show items with no data" OFF, so the Calc rows
//      do not sprout empty children.

PL Value :=
VAR _Line = SELECTEDVALUE ( 'PL Layout'[PL Line] )
VAR _Type = SELECTEDVALUE ( 'PL Layout'[Row Type] )
VAR _IsDrilled = ISINSCOPE ( TBData[PL Level 2] )
RETURN
    IF (
        // a Calc row has no detail to break down -- blank it when drilled
        _Type = "Calc" && _IsDrilled,
        BLANK (),
        SWITCH (
            _Line,
            "Revenue",         CALCULATE ( [Amount], TBData[PL Level 1] = "Revenue" ),
            "COGS",            CALCULATE ( [Amount], TBData[PL Level 1] = "COGS" ),
            "OPEX",            CALCULATE ( [Amount], TBData[PL Level 1] = "OPEX" ),
            "Total Revenue",   [Total Revenue],
            "Gross Profit",    [Gross Profit],
            "Adjusted EBITDA", [Adjusted EBITDA],
            BLANK ()
        )
    )

// How the filtering works: PL Level 2 and PL Level 3 are columns ON TBData,
// so when the matrix drills into them they filter TBData directly. The
// CALCULATE in each Section branch then narrows that to the right section.
// PL Layout contributes only the row label -- it filters nothing, which is
// exactly why the Calc rows can reach across sections.
//
// Grand total: SELECTEDVALUE returns BLANK when several PL Lines are in
// scope, so the total row falls to the final BLANK(). If you want Adjusted
// EBITDA on the total row instead, replace that last BLANK() with
// [Adjusted EBITDA].
